    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Arguments for maintaining Chevron deference often center on providing agency expertise and predictability in administrative law. Proponents argue that courts should defer to federal agencies best equipped to interpret complex statutes. Conversely, critics contend that Chevron allows agencies to substitute their policy preferences for Congress's clear intent. The validity of concerns rests on whether the agency is truly \"best equipped\" or if the deference leads to judicial abdication of proper legal review.",
